 The dev console's Agent Manager, driven through the key handler the
 console actually calls.

 Every key here goes through `handle-console-input`, and that is the
 point rather than a detail. ModeAgent was missing from its `when`, so it
 fell to `is otherwise -> state` and the Agent Manager swallowed every
 keystroke including Back: the only exit was a reboot. A test that called
 `dispatch-agent-menu` directly would have passed against that bug with
 nothing wired up, which is how the bug survived being written down in
 CurrentPlan as a plain gap for months.

 What the menu answered before was three literals. "Registry: 0 agents"
 was the reply on a machine with a signed agent on its boot image and on
 a machine with nothing, in identical words. So the load line below is
 the load-bearing one: this runs against the same honest bundle disk as
 `codex/test/apps/bundled-agent`, the registry count moves from empty to
 one, and the agent's own name and version come back out of it. A console
 still answering from a literal cannot produce "codex-demo-agent v0.1.0"
 because it has never read the disk.

 The role swap is checked by listing twice. `list` prints the role, the
 two listings straddle a switch to code-review, and they differ. Without
 the role on the line a swap that did nothing would print the same text
 twice and read as success.

 Both refusals matter as much as the successes. Switching a role with an
 empty registry must say so rather than report the new role, or the
 console would be telling the operator it had done something to nothing;
 and an unknown key must leave the status untouched, or every stray
 keystroke would overwrite the last real answer.

 THE STRAY-KEY CHECK COMPARES TEXT, AND IT HAS TO. `__record-set` is an
 in-place field store that hands back the SAME record, which the compiler
 documents in `Types/TypeEnv.codex` and relies on itself, so every state
 in this file is one object that each dispatch mutates as it passes
 through. There is no earlier copy to hold a stray key against. Written
 the obvious way -- dispatch the stray key, compare the result with the
 state it came from -- the check would compare a record with itself and
 pass unconditionally. It got written that way first and reported the
 status of a LATER dispatch, which is what exposed this. Capturing the
 status Text before the keystroke is the version that can fail, because
 Text is a value and does not travel with the record.

 We say:

Section: Fixtures

 A console state with no UEFI underneath it. Nothing in this test prints
 through the console record, so its handles are zero rather than
 something that must be pretended into existence, and the fact store is
 empty rather than loaded off a disk.

  bare-console : UefiConsoleState
  bare-console = UefiConsoleState {
    sys-table = 0,
    con-out = 0,
    con-in = 0,
    rows = 25,
    cols = 80,
    cursor-row = 0,
    cursor-col = 0
  }

  bare-disk : DiskFactStore
  bare-disk = DiskFactStore { superblock = empty-superblock, mem = fact-store-empty, base = 0, limit = 0 }

  bare-keys : Keypair
  bare-keys = generate-keypair (sha256 (text-to-bytes "codex-agent-manager-test"))

Section: Reporting

  mode-name : ConsoleMode -> Text
  mode-name (m) = when m
    is ModeMainMenu -> "main"
    is ModeAgent -> "agent"
    is otherwise -> "other"

  report : Text, DevConsoleState -> [Console] Nothing
  report (label) (s) = act
    print-line-uni (label & ": " & s.status)
  end

  report-where : Text, DevConsoleState -> [Console] Nothing
  report-where (label) (s) = act
    print-line-uni (label & ": mode=" & mode-name (s.mode) & " menu=" & s.menu.title)
  end

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console, Device.Port, Device.Block] Nothing = act
    let s0 = dev-console-init bare-console bare-disk bare-keys
    in let s1 = dispatch-main-menu "agent" s0
    in act
      report-where "entered" s1

      empty <- handle-console-input s1 "list"
      report "list-empty" empty

      rolemenu0 <- handle-console-input s1 "role"
      no-agent <- handle-console-input rolemenu0 "review"
      report "role-no-agent" no-agent

      loaded <- handle-console-input s1 "load"
      report "load" loaded

      listed <- handle-console-input loaded "list"
      report "list-loaded" listed

      inspected <- handle-console-input listed "status"
      report "status" inspected

      rolemenu <- handle-console-input listed "role"
      report-where "role-menu" rolemenu

      swapped <- handle-console-input rolemenu "review"
      report "swap" swapped
      report-where "after-swap" swapped

      relisted <- handle-console-input swapped "list"
      report "list-swapped" relisted

      let held = relisted.status
      in act
        stray <- handle-console-input relisted "nonsense"
        print-line-uni ("stray-untouched: " & show (stray.status == held))
      end

      probed <- handle-console-input relisted "gpu"
      report "gpu" probed

      out <- handle-console-input relisted "back"
      report-where "back" out
    end
  end
